export const RUBRIC_VERSION = "1.4";
export const RUBRIC_TEXT = "# Rating Rubric v1.4\n\nThis file is the single source of truth for scoring. v1.1 (2026-11-25) added the Sierra and Mizuki checklists. v1.2 (2026-11-26) takes strengths from moments instead of category scores (honesty rule 6); scoring is unchanged. v1.3 (2026-11-26) defines when a death is avoidable, makes every fix name its situation, and adds habit checks; the score anchors are unchanged. v1.4 (2026-11-26) gives every hero in the game a mechanics checklist (HERO_CHECKLISTS.md); the anchors are unchanged. The analyzer system prompts embed it verbatim, and the version number is stored with every report. Changing it later bumps the version, and old reports keep the version they were scored with.\n\n## Scale and what the numbers mean\n\nEvery category is scored 1–10 **or `not assessable`**. The bands are tied to rank so that a score means something concrete:\n\n| Score | Level the play looks like |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Below typical Bronze |\n| 3–4 | Bronze–Silver |\n| 5–6 | Gold–Platinum |\n| 7–8 | Diamond–Master |\n| 9–10 | Grandmaster–Champion |\n\nThe rank mapping is a coaching heuristic built from community coaching guides (see PLAN.md §1.4). It is not a statistical model. The app never claims \"this is exactly Gold play\". It says \"this matches the Gold–Platinum anchor\".\n\n**Scoring rules**\n- Score against the anchors below only. Never score against \"effort\" or \"for a Bronze player\".\n- A score needs **≥ 2 independent pieces of evidence** (timestamps). With fewer, the category is `not assessable`.\n- A score of 7 or higher needs ≥ 3 timestamps of the anchor behaviour **and** no counter-evidence of the 3–4 anchor in the same match.\n- Confidence for each finding is one of:\n  - `high`: directly visible in ≥ 2 frames, or the OCR timeline and a frame agree.\n  - `medium`: visible in one frame, or inferred from the OCR timeline alone.\n  - `low`: inferred from partial view or context.\n- Low-confidence findings never enter the \"3 costliest mistakes\".\n\n## Overall score\n\nWeighted mean of the assessable categories, with weights renormalised over those that were assessed. If fewer than 5 categories are assessable, the overall score is `not assessable`.\n\n| Category | DPS weight | Support weight |\n|---|---|---|\n| Aim | 14 | 8 |\n| Movement | 8 | 8 |\n| Positioning | 15 | 16 |\n| Engagement & tempo | 12 | 12 |\n| Target priority (Support: resource priority) | 10 | 14 |\n| Cooldown discipline | 9 | 10 |\n| Ultimate economy | 8 | 10 |\n| Game sense & awareness | 8 | 8 |\n| Death quality | 12 | 10 |\n| Hero mechanics | 4 | 4 |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Aim\n**Definition:** Crosshair placement before contact, tracking and flicking during contact, and shot discipline (range, recoil, rail charge).\n**Observable:** Crosshair height relative to enemy heads in consecutive frames, pre-aim on likely peek corners, crosshair staying on a strafing target, hit markers or damage numbers if the replay HUD shows them, Sojourn rail fired at low charge.\n**Not assessable when:** No frame sequence ≥ 5 fps exists around the duel, the footage is third-person or free-cam, or compression blur hides the crosshair.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Crosshair mostly at the floor or walls, far from where enemies appear. Fires at targets far outside effective range. No sustained tracking in most duels. |\n| 3–4 | Placement is reactive: chest or feet height, snapping only after the enemy appears. Tracking breaks when the target or you strafe. Rail and Helix shots go to body or tank. |\n| 5–6 | Usually head height on common angles. Tracks predictable movement consistently. Wins most even duels against slow targets and loses to mobile flankers. |\n| 7–8 | Pre-aims likely peeks. Holds head-level tracking while strafing under pressure. Lands key headshots on high-value targets. |\n| 9–10 | First-shot accuracy and placement near perfect. Wins duels against mobile heroes. Aim holds while dodging. |\n\n**Bronze → Gold → Master:** Bronze aims at whatever is closest or biggest and either stops moving to aim or moves without aiming. Gold tracks easy targets but falls apart under pressure. In Master, crosshair placement does half the work, and headshots land on the most valuable target while moving.\n\n## 2. Movement\n**Definition:** Movement in duels (strafing, crouch, jumps), cover-to-cover travel, mobility abilities (Sojourn slide, Soldier sprint, other heroes' mobility) and rotation paths.\n**Observable:** Position change across frame sequences, time spent in open ground between cover, mobility icon state before and after an engagement, walking in straight lines into sightlines.\n**Not assessable when:** Only isolated keyframes exist (this needs sequences ≥ 3 fps).\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Walks in straight lines into enemy sightlines and stands still while shooting. Mobility used at random. |\n| 3–4 | Strafe is predictable or missing. Uses mobility to engage and then has no escape. Rotates through the open main path. |\n| 5–6 | Strafes in duels and mostly moves cover to cover. Sometimes spends mobility without a reason. |\n| 7–8 | Unpredictable duel movement. Keeps an escape available. Uses off-paths to rotate. |\n| 9–10 | Movement consistently denies enemy aim. Always has an exit. |\n\n**Bronze → Gold → Master:** Bronze walks the main path and stops to shoot. Gold strafes in duels but still overextends with mobility. Master syncs movement with cover and cooldowns and is rarely caught without an escape.\n\n## 3. Positioning\n**Definition:** Where you stand relative to cover, high ground, enemy sightlines, your team (spacing), and your healers' line of sight, in each fight phase.\n**Observable:** Cover within a step, elevation, number of enemies with visible line of sight, teammates on screen, distance to team, and frames 3–10 s before each death.\n**Not assessable when:** The frames don't show the surroundings, for example free-cam or scoped views.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Routinely in the open with multiple enemy sightlines, far from the team. |\n| 3–4 | Plays in front of the tank or on the main path, with no cover within one step. Position doesn't change with fight state. |\n| 5–6 | Mostly near cover and uses some high ground. Sometimes too far forward or isolated from healing. |\n| 7–8 | Takes off-angles that create crossfire while keeping an escape and healer line of sight. |\n| 9–10 | Always in the strongest available spot for the fight phase. The enemy has to commit resources to reach you. |\n\n**Bronze → Gold → Master:** Bronze stands in front of the tank on the main path. Gold knows the common high-ground spots but holds them too long. Master rotates between angles and times off-angles with the team's engage.\n\n## 4. Engagement & tempo (decision-making, teamwork timing)\n**Definition:** When to engage or disengage relative to the fight state (5v5, 4v5), staggering, regrouping, and fighting with the team instead of alone.\n**Observable:** Kill feed counts at the moment you engage, whether you walk in alone after a team wipe, whether you fall back after a lost fight, and teammates on screen when you take a duel.\n**Not assessable when:** The kill feed is unreadable and the team state can't be reconstructed.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Fights regardless of numbers. Staggers in one by one after wipes. Chases kills into the enemy spawn. |\n| 3–4 | Engages when it looks fun rather than when numbers favour it. Rarely disengages. Joins fights late or alone. |\n| 5–6 | Generally plays with the team. Sometimes takes bad 1v2s or stays in a lost fight too long. |\n| 7–8 | Engages on advantages (picks, enemy cooldowns spent). Disengages early from lost fights and regroups. |\n| 9–10 | Dictates tempo: creates the advantage, converts it, and practically never staggers. |\n\n**Bronze → Gold → Master:** Bronze has no sense of fight state and runs straight back in alone from spawn. Gold knows to group but holds on too long. Master tracks numbers and cooldowns, disengages lost fights and saves resources.\n\n## 5. Target priority (Support: resource priority)\n**Definition:** DPS: shooting the target that wins the fight, meaning low HP, isolated, supports or squishies, or the enemy's carry, and confirming kills. Support: who gets healing and utility, and when to deal damage instead.\n**Observable:** Who the crosshair is on across frames, kill-feed victims, and damage-to-elims ratio from the scoreboard (high damage with few elims points at tank or shield damage).\n**Not assessable when:** No contact frames exist and no scoreboard was entered.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Shoots whatever is closest, mostly the tank or barriers. |\n| 3–4 | Damage goes mostly into the tank or shields. Switches targets constantly. Lets low targets escape. |\n| 5–6 | Often focuses squishies but loses focus under pressure. |\n| 7–8 | Consistent priority (supports, isolated, low HP) that lines up with the team's focus. |\n| 9–10 | Always picks the fight-winning target. Frequently gets the first pick. |\n\n## 6. Cooldown discipline\n**Definition:** Timing and purpose of abilities. Live tools (escapes, self-heal, defensives) are held for threats. Win tools (burst, crowd control) are used when the team can follow up. No waste and no stacking.\n**Observable:** Ability icons in the HUD (greyed out or showing a timer), ability use relative to deaths, and whether a death happens with an escape or heal still available.\n**Not assessable when:** The ability HUD isn't visible or readable in the footage.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Abilities used at random or not at all. Dies with escape or heal available. |\n| 3–4 | Uses mobility to engage and then dies without it. Panic heals. Holds abilities through whole fights. |\n| 5–6 | Mostly sensible. Occasionally stacks or wastes cooldowns. |\n| 7–8 | Holds live tools for real threats. Combines win tools with the team. |\n| 9–10 | Baits and tracks enemy cooldowns. Never dies with a relevant cooldown up. |\n\n## 7. Ultimate economy\n**Definition:** Charge efficiency, timing (fight-winning versus \"donating\" into a lost fight), combining with team ults, not overlapping ults, not ulting into an obvious counter.\n**Observable:** Your ult % over time and the top bar's ult % for all 10 players (OCR), ult activation frames, kill feed in the 5 s after an ult, and the team state at the moment of the ult (including enemy ults ready).\n**Not assessable when:** The ult meter can't be read and no activation is visible.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Sits on ult for minutes, or ults with no targets or after the fight is lost. |\n| 3–4 | Ults into lost fights, overlaps with teammates' ults, or ults only the tank. Low charge rate. |\n| 5–6 | Usually ults inside fights. Sometimes wasteful or late. |\n| 7–8 | Uses ult to open or win fights at the right moment. Good charge rate. |\n| 9–10 | Every ult is decisive or deliberately saved. Tracks enemy ults. |\n\n## 8. Game sense & awareness\n**Definition:** Tracking enemy positions and flankers, respecting visible and audible threats, reacting to cues, and anticipating enemy ults.\n**Observable:**\n- Turning toward a flanker before taking damage, deaths from behind, visible enemies ignored while tunnelling.\n- Footsteps or enemy ult voice lines in the audio followed by no reaction.\n- Enemy ult % in the top bar near 100 % before you commit.\n\n**Not assessable when:** The video has no audio track, in which case only visual cues are scored. Voice comms are never scored, because replays don't contain them.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Repeatedly killed from behind without turning. Ignores visible enemy ults. |\n| 3–4 | Tunnels on one target and is surprised by flanks. |\n| 5–6 | Checks some flanks. Sometimes caught by known threats. |\n| 7–8 | Anticipates flanks and enemy ults. |\n| 9–10 | Rarely surprised. |\n\n## 9. Death quality (fed by the Death review agent)\n**Definition:** How often you die, whether you die first in fights, how many deaths were avoidable, and what each death bought (a trade, objective time).\n**Observable:** Kill feed, death frames with 10 s pre-roll, and the per-death classification below.\n**Not assessable when:** Fewer than 2 deaths could be located.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | First death in most fights. Nearly all deaths avoidable. No value traded. |\n| 3–4 | Frequent first deaths. Most deaths avoidable. Little value traded. |\n| 5–6 | Some first deaths. About half avoidable. |\n| 7–8 | Rarely the first death. Deaths are mostly trades or unavoidable. |\n| 9–10 | Dies rarely, and nearly every death has value. |\n\nThe anchors use shares measured inside the match (first-death share, avoidable share). They don't use population benchmarks such as \"Bronze averages X deaths\", because no source for those numbers was found.\n\n**Per-death classification (every death):**\n- `cause`: one of\n  - positioning\n  - timing (stagger or bad engage)\n  - cooldown\n  - awareness (flank or off-angle)\n  - mechanics (lost a fair duel)\n  - unavoidable\n  - unknown\n- `avoidable`: one of\n  - yes: you can name one specific action the player could have taken in the ~5 s before the death that would likely have kept them alive, and `whatToDoInstead` names it.\n  - no: a trade that won the fight or bought objective time, a threat the player couldn't see or react to in time (for example an ultimate from out of view), or a death while playing the role correctly.\n  - unclear: the footage doesn't show enough to tell.\n  \"Positioning could always be better\" is not enough for yes: name the action.\n- `firstDeathOfFight`: true or false.\n- `whatToDoInstead`: one concrete alternative that follows the fix rule below.\n- Confidence and timestamp.\n\nThe review starts about 10 s before the death frame, because the mistake usually happens before the moment of death.\n\n## 10. Hero mechanics\n**Definition:** A hero-specific checklist. The checklists are versioned per patch, because kits and perks change.\n\n**Checklists:** every hero in the game has one, in `HERO_CHECKLISTS.md` (version 2026-11-26), 5–6 items each. They were written from the official ability and perk lists on Blizzard's hero pages, so ability names match the current game. Examples:\n- **Sojourn:** building rail energy with primary fire, headshot rails, slide-jump, Disruptor placement, Overclock timing.\n- **Soldier: 76:** burst fire at range, Helix plus primary combo, Biotic Field (or Stim Pack) placement, Sprint rotations, Visor timing.\n- **Ana:** Sleep Dart held for divers or channelled ultimates, anti-heal on the focused target, Nano Boost on an ally who can use it.\n- The newest heroes' pages don't say which ability is the ultimate, so their checklists don't either.\n- The checklists of the heroes played are added to the prompt. A perk that replaces an ability is judged as the perk version. A hero without a checklist (one added to the game after this version) is scored `not assessable`.\n\n| Score | Anchor |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | Core kit unused. |\n| 3–4 | Basic use. Misses key interactions. |\n| 5–6 | Competent core kit. Advanced techniques missing. |\n| 7–8 | Advanced techniques used consistently. |\n| 9–10 | Optimal kit usage. |\n\n---\n\n## Fixes\n\nEvery fix, and every `whatToDoInstead`, names the situation and the action: when it applies (the trigger) and what to do instead (a position, a timing or a target). Use the map and the moment when they are known.\n- Good: \"When your Reinhardt walks through the choke, wait behind the left pillar until the enemy shoots at him, then peek from the pillar.\"\n- Not enough: \"Use cover.\" \"Stay with your team.\" \"Position better.\"\n\n## Habit checks\n\nWhen the player is training one habit, the prompt names it with its trigger and action. The specialist that owns the habit's category marks every moment the trigger comes up in the footage: the timestamp, whether the player did the habit, and what is visible then. Only moments where the trigger is visible count. Habit checks never change a score, and they are not findings.\n\n## Honesty rules (embedded verbatim in every analyzer system prompt)\n\n1. Deadly honest. No sugarcoating, no filler praise, no \"great job, but\". A bad match is called bad.\n2. Honest also means accurate: never invent a mistake or a stat. Every criticism is backed by a timestamp or data, and low confidence is stated.\n3. Score strictly against the rubric anchors. Bronze-level play gets Bronze-level scores. No grade inflation.\n4. Tone of a strict pro coach: critique the play, not the person.\n5. If something is not visible in the footage, output `not assessable` instead of guessing.\n6. A strength is a moment where the play matches its category's 5–6 anchor or higher, with the evidence visible at that timestamp. Strengths are reported only when the same category shows ≥ 2 such moments at medium or high confidence, whatever the category's score: the score judges the whole match, a strength names a habit worth keeping. Strengths never raise a score. If nothing qualifies, the strengths list is empty.\n\n**Banned phrases (the validator fails the report if any appear):**\n- Filler praise: \"great job\", \"good job\", \"well done\", \"nice work\", \"good effort\", \"keep it up\", \"you're doing great\", \"don't be too hard on yourself\", \"solid game overall\", \"overall not bad\", \"amazing\", \"awesome\", \"fantastic\", \"impressive\", \"proud of you\".\n- Attacks on the person rather than the play: \"you suck\", \"you're bad\", \"trash\", \"hopeless\", \"no talent\".\n";
